{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-01-cost-price-selling-price-profit-loss",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Cost Price, Selling Price, Profit, and Loss",
    "description": "Build the basic profit-and-loss model, keep cost price as the percentage base, and solve for any missing value.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Define cost price (CP), selling price (SP), profit and loss, and derive their relationships from the difference SP−CP or CP−SP. Establish that ordinary profit and loss percentages are measured against CP, not SP. Convert between CP, SP, and a stated profit/loss rate, including reverse questions where CP or SP is unknown. Include zero-profit boundary, exact fractional rates, currency units, and substitution checks. Ground examples in the chapter's opening formulae and solved examples 1–9."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cost Price, Selling Price, Profit, and Loss: Build the basic profit-and-loss model, keep cost price as the percentage base, and solve for any missing value. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A transaction has two central amounts: cost price (CP), what the seller gives up to acquire or make the article, and selling price (SP), what the seller receives. Compare these amounts before naming the result.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If SP exceeds CP, profit=SP−CP. If SP is below CP, loss=CP−SP. When they are equal, there is neither profit nor loss. The difference is an amount of money, not yet a percentage.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Ordinary profit percent is profit divided by CP, times 100. Loss percent is loss divided by CP, times 100. CP is the investment at risk, so it is the base unless the question explicitly defines another measure.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: buy for ₹800 and sell for ₹920. Profit=920−800=₹120. Relative to the ₹800 cost, 120/800=0.15, so profit is 15%. Dividing by ₹920 would answer a different comparison.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To move from CP to SP, use a factor. A gain of g% gives SP=CP(1+g/100); a loss of l% gives SP=CP(1−l/100). A 20% gain means 120% of cost, while a 20% loss means 80%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Reverse the relationship by dividing. If SP=₹1,080 after a 20% gain, then 1.2CP=1,080 and CP=1,080/1.2=₹900. Substitution checks that 20% of 900 is 180 and the receipt is 1,080.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If SP is known after a loss, the retained fraction matters. At a 15% loss, SP is 85% of CP; therefore CP=SP/0.85. Do not subtract 15% from SP, because the loss rate was measured on CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If CP and SP are given, determine the sign before calculating the rate. This avoids reporting a loss as profit merely because the absolute difference is positive.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use exact fractions where they simplify the calculation. A 25% profit factor is 5/4; a 20% loss factor is 4/5. Fraction form keeps intermediate money exact when decimals repeat.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The break-even boundary is a useful check: zero profit corresponds to SP=CP and 0%. Positive gain requires SP>CP; a negative signed result can be described as loss, but ordinary loss percent is usually stated as a positive magnitude.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep currency labels attached to amounts. Profit is rupees; profit percent is a rate. Adding ₹100 to 10% or comparing 10% directly with ₹100 mixes unlike quantities.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When a later selling price is requested for a target gain, first recover CP if it is not known. Then apply the target multiplier to that same CP; do not apply the new target rate to an old SP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A proportional check: doubling CP and keeping the same percentage margin doubles profit and SP. If the calculated profit does not scale this way, revisit which amount is the base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use a compact ledger with CP, SP, difference, and rate. It is especially helpful when a story includes two possible sale prices or a change from loss to profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Before finalizing, state the requested quantity precisely: CP, SP, profit amount, loss amount, profit percentage, or loss percentage. The same data can ask for any of these distinct results.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
